{"reference":"Job 2","book":"Job","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The heavenly scene","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"In Job 1–2, the Hebrew expression hasatan means “the accuser” or “the adversary.” Its article suggests a role within this heavenly assembly rather than an unambiguous personal name for the later devil. The chapter portrays this figure appearing among the sons of God before YHWH.","verse_refs":["Job 2:1-7","Job 2:11-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_002_7efccd783a99132de7b7423c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Job is explicitly described as blameless, upright, God-fearing, and turned away from evil before his suffering; YHWH repeats that assessment here. The narrative therefore does not let readers treat his calamity as evidence of hidden wrongdoing.","verse_refs":["Job 2:11-13","Job 2:3"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_002_b1981b412cf93d2a29010659"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"What the reader knows","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The heavenly scene gives readers knowledge that Job and his companions do not have: his testing is framed before YHWH, while those on earth confront its effects without that explanation. This difference helps distinguish the narrative’s wider perspective from the limited understanding of its suffering characters.","verse_refs":["Job 2:1-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_002_a6012810cec758b3583bf3fd"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"The friends’ first response","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar begin by mourning with Job in silence for seven days, because his grief is overwhelming. Later in the book, their attempts to explain his suffering become harmful when they infer guilt and treat suffering as proof of wrongdoing. Their initial presence should therefore not be confused with an endorsement of all their later speeches.","verse_refs":["Job 2:1-7","Job 2:11-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_002_7efccd783a99132de7b7423c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}